I ascended for the third time now.
I wanted to play as a pacifist but failed because of an incident
between a tin opener and a gnome zombie.
This started well when I found a blessed Fire brand on a bones file
early in the mines.  This proved an extremely useful way to earn
protection: Sell Fire brand, go buy protection, come back and let your
pet steal it. Repeat and rinse until you bump into a gnome zombie and
lose your conduct.
Praying yielded Magicbane, which was my choice weapon until I found
the Staff of Aesculapius. The Cyclops was everything but a challenge.
A wimp. Maybe even easier than Vlad.
Medusa proved to be a problem. While there, having only boots of
jumping, I fell through a hole, right into the castle level. I was not
really ready for the castle, so I went upstairs, thinking don't forget
to put your blindfold". Thinking and doing are, unfortunately, two
different things. I went up, saw my pet turned into a statue and was
relieved that the uncursed amulet I had been picking had been an AoLS,
since Medusa was the level exactly above the Castle. I had never seen
that and thought there must be some levels in between... Wheew, very
close to YASD here.
My pet was, at that time, a pit fiend. He had been a cat and a dragon
before, but polymorph traps and chameleon corpses being what they are,
he became a quite powerful devil. Until Fort Ludios that is. There, he
fell into a pit. I pushed him out of the pit and that made him become
feral. He gated in a few other demons, including Juiblex (a wand of
digging mostly got rid of this one). Having to fight 5 or 6 pit fiends
when you just had cleared the level is, well, errr... Remind me to
send my pet away next time I go to Ludios.
Later on, Taperapas would have up to 7 pet dragons, but he lost some
of them to traps in Gehennom and found them too slow and burdensome to
take them with him. They did do one thing for him, though, which was
killing all priests BEFORE he could pay them lump sums for some
protection.
Ended up with naked AC of -5 anyway, never got crowned. Wished for
GDSM, GoD, HoB, a T-shirt and scrolls of charging (the wand of wishing
had only 1 charge, found some magic lamps, but only used one as a
light source).
I found a ring of conflict late in the game and didn't use it much. I
made Ludious without it, which was a premiere for me.
I also saw Rodney's first comeback before I had reached the sanctum or
performed the ritual. I used my wand of Death on him, and later
recharged it to make sure he'd not bothe me again.
Overall, Rodney was not too much of a pain, and the mysterious force
was bearable. The arch priest of Moloch was dispatched thanks to some
conflict and to the Staff. He was quite easy too, though he did hurt
me a bit.
The plane of Earth was piece of cake. Air was about draining life of
air elementals before they pummelled me to death, and resting in steam
and energy vortices. The portal was the farthest possible, but overall
it was not too hard, particularly considering I didn't have any potion
of healing of any kind with me, and my power wouldn't come back very
fast with the Amulet, so casting healing spells worked but not very
ling. Fire was extraordinarily easy. For someone who once got stuck in
lava and once died there because of a *** archon who kept summoning
hordes of monsters, it was very refreshing. Water was also very easy.
The astral plane was tricky. Famine to the right, Death in the middle.
I went right, teleported away those pesky priests, killed some a's,
zapped a wand of death a few times at Famine, teleported it away and
found out this was the priest of Poseidon. I went out, killed Famine
twice with my wand, teleported everyone out of the way as much as I
could, whistled my angel, and moved on. I wanted to bypass Death and
go to the left. No luck, a stupid knight opened the middle door. I
killed him, and ran: advance, shrug off a few magic missiles, teleport
Death away, run, shrug some more spells, advance, run, breathe when a
vagrant and a priest stand between Death and me, open the door,
teleport the priest standing in the way, dodge more magic missiles,
discover this is Hermes' altar.

